body:
### Description:

When using add_flag with default values (e.g. --mode-a{0},--mode-b{1},--mode-c{2}) bound to an integer/enum variable, config file serialization via config_to_str() and subsequent parsing via set_config() does not behave consistently with CLI behavior.

### Expected behavior:

A flag like --mode-a (with default value {0}) set via CLI should produce a config file entry that, when read back, results in the same value being stored - i.e. the flag name should be sufficient to identify which value to apply, and the config value should be treated as a boolean presence indicator.

### Actual behavior:

config_to_str() serializes the current value of the bound variable as the config value, e.g.:

```ini
mode-a=0
mode-b=1
```

When reading back, CLI11 takes the value after = literally and writes it directly to the bound variable, bypassing the flag's baked-in default entirely. This means:

mode-a=2 sets the variable to 2, not 0, so the key name is effectively meaningless
mode-a=0 accidentally works, but only because the literal value happens to match the baked-in default
A user hand-editing the config file and writing mode-a=true or mode-a=1 gets an unexpected result

The "{}" magic value that triggers the baked-in default path in get_flag_value / _add_flag_like_result is never produced by the config file parser, bare keys without = are parsed as =true (i.e. "1"), so the baked in default is always ignored.

The correct behavior would be for config_to_str() to output mode-a=true (or just mode-a) for a flag that was set, and the config parser should treat any truthy value as "this flag was passed, apply its baked-in default." This would make the config file format consistent and human-editable.

### Reproduction:
```cpp
CLI::App app;
int mode = 0;
auto* group = app.add_option_group("Mode")->require_option(1);
group->add_flag("--mode-a{0},--mode-b{1},--mode-c{2}", mode, "Operation mode");

// Parse from CLI: --mode-c => mode == 2
// config_to_str() outputs: mode-c=2
// Parse from config with mode-c=2 => mode == 2 (ok)
// Parse from config with mode-c=0 => mode == 0 (wrong, should be 2)
// Parse from config with mode-c=true => mode == 1 (wrong, should be 2)
```
